  "nameStory": "The Red-tailed Mining Bee gets its name from the reddish coloration on its abdomen, which looks like a rusty-red tail when seen from behind. The 'mining' part comes from the female's habit of digging tunnels in the ground to create underground nests for her eggs, much like a tiny miner working underground.",
  "description": "The Red-tailed Mining Bee is a medium-sized solitary bee that's part of New England's diverse native bee community. These fuzzy, industrious bees are excellent pollinators that spend their lives visiting flowers and digging underground nests. Females are typically around half an inch long with dark bodies and distinctive reddish-orange coloring on their abdomens. Unlike honeybees that live in large colonies, these bees live alone and are generally quite gentle around humans.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Reddish abdomen",
      "description": "The most striking feature is the rusty-red to orange coloration on the abdomen (belly area), which contrasts sharply with the darker head and thorax",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Fuzzy body texture",
      "description": "The entire body is covered in soft, dense hairs that give it a velvety appearance and help collect pollen while foraging",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Ground nesting behavior",
      "description": "Look for small piles of excavated soil near pencil-width holes in bare or sparsely vegetated ground where females dig their tunnels",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Size comparison",
      "description": "About the size of a small honeybee but more robust and stocky in build, with a noticeably rounder abdomen",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Flight pattern",
      "description": "Flies with a purposeful, slightly buzzy sound, often hovering briefly over flowers before landing to forage",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Red-tailed Mining Bees prefer areas with well-drained, sandy or loamy soils where females can easily excavate their nesting tunnels. Look for them in sunny open spaces like meadows, field edges, gardens, and even sparse lawns where wildflowers are present. They're often found near their preferred food sources including spring wildflowers, fruit tree blossoms, and native plants.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "Peak activity time when adults emerge and begin foraging on early spring flowers like cherry blossoms and dandelions. This is the best time to spot them and observe their distinctive red abdomens.",
    "summer": "Adult activity continues into early summer, with females actively digging nests and provisioning them with pollen balls. Look for small soil mounds marking nest entrances.",
    "fall": "Adults have typically finished their life cycle by fall, but new generation may be developing underground in their natal tunnels.",
    "winter": "Immature bees overwinter underground in their nest tunnels, completely hidden from view until the following spring emergence."
  },
  "funFact": "Each female Red-tailed Mining Bee is essentially a single mom who builds an entire underground apartment complex by herself, creating multiple branching tunnels with individual rooms where she stocks pollen 'meatballs' and lays eggs - she can move 40 times her body weight in soil during nest construction!",
  "confusionNotes": "Most easily confused with other reddish mining bees in the same genus, but the Red-tailed Mining Bee's abdomen is more consistently and vibrantly orange-red compared to others that may have just reddish tinges. Remember: if the whole 'tail end' looks like it was dipped in rust, you've likely found your red-tailed miner.",
  "contextualKnowledge": "Mining bees get their name from their excavation behavior, not because they're found near mines or mineral deposits. These are some of our most important native pollinators, often more efficient than honeybees at pollinating native plants, yet they're frequently overlooked because they don't make honey or live in obvious hives.",
  "compareHints": "Among the many Andrena mining bees in New England, the Red-tailed Mining Bee stands out for its particularly vibrant and extensive reddish coloration on the abdomen. While species like the Hairy-banded Mining Bee have distinct hair bands and others may have subtle reddish tinges, erythrogaster displays the most consistent and noticeable red 'tail' coloration. The cherry and hawthorn mining bees may be seen on similar flowers but lack the bold abdominal coloring.",
